                <h2>'Running' Doesn't Mean 'Selling'</h2>
<p>Most overseas advertisers are familiar with the term 'creative lifespan,' but few take time to distinguish: is your creative 'dead from exhaustion' or 'dead from no sales'? </p>
<p>These sound similar but yield completely different outcomes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Running</strong>: The creative gets impressions, clicks, and spends budget—账户 runs without errors.<br />
<strong>Selling</strong>: The creative not only gets clicks but drives effective conversions—signups, purchases, add-to-cart, whatever your goal is.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Most teams only complete the first step in their reviews—checking if creatives can run. But what truly determines ROI is whether they can sell.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: What Does a 'Running' Creative Look Like?</h2>
<p>Simply put, it's a creative that has passed Meta's learning phase and achieved stable impressions and clicks.</p>
<p>Typical characteristics:</p>
<ul>
<li>CPM hovers around category average—not too high</li>
<li>CTR exceeds 1%, sometimes reaching 2-3%</li>
<li>Account maintains steady spend without being throttled for low quality scores</li>
</ul>
<p>These creatives usually have strong visual impact, capture attention in the first 3 seconds, and feature clear CTAs. They feel rewarding to run—until you check the conversion data.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: What Does a 'Selling' Creative Look Like?</h2>
<p>A 'selling' creative adds conversion-layer validation on top of 'running' metrics:</p>
<ul>
<li>CVR (click-to-conversion rate) meets or exceeds category benchmark</li>
<li>CPA falls within acceptable range relative to target LTV</li>
<li>User behavior path is complete—not just landing and bouncing</li>
</ul>
<p>A creative that sells means it not only catches attention but accurately communicates product value, prompting target users to take action.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: Why Do Many Creatives Run But Don't Sell?</h2>
<p>This is the most common issue. A utility app team once ran an experiment: launching 20 creatives simultaneously. Twelve achieved spend, but only three ultimately brought paying users.</p>
<p><strong>Common reasons for high CTR but low CVR:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Disconnect between hook and landing</strong>—The video promotes Feature A in the first 3 seconds, but the product page showcases Feature B. Users click in expecting one thing, find something else, and bounce immediately.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Audience targeting misalignment</strong>—The creative attracts curious browsers rather than users with genuine need. CTR looks great, but conversion intent is zero.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Unclear value proposition</strong>—Emphasis on discounts, promotions, or 'free' without explaining 'why it's worth buying.' Users are hooked but find no reason to open their wallets.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Poor landing page experience</strong>—The creative might be fine, but post-click page loads slowly, forms are complex, or checkout flows are cumbersome. Users drop at the final step.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: How to Determine If a Creative Can Run or Sell During Review?</h2>
<p>Use this three-dimensional framework:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Running</th>
<th>Selling</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Impression → Click</td>
<td>CTR ≥ 1%</td>
<td>Reasonable CTR, not artificially inflated</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Click → Conversion</td>
<td>CVR ≥ category average</td>
<td>CVR ≥ category average, stable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cost → Revenue</td>
<td>CPA acceptable</td>
<td>CPA ÷ LTV &lt; target payback period</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If your creative has 2% CTR but 0.3% CVR—well below the category benchmark of 0.8%—it's likely running but not selling.</p>
<p><strong>Pull all three layers of data during reviews.</strong> Don't just look at spend and clicks.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: What to Do When a Creative Runs But Doesn't Sell?</h2>
<p>Don't rush to pause the ad. Take these three steps first:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Check the landing page</strong>—70% of the time, the problem originates here. Review Pixel feed data to identify where users drop off.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Split audience reports</strong>—If the same creative sells to Audience A but not Audience B, it's a targeting issue, not a creative issue.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>A/B test hook-landing consistency</strong>—If the video promises 'learn a new language in 3 minutes,' the landing page must immediately reinforce that promise, not dump product features.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>If troubleshooting still doesn't work, the creative itself is the problem. Move on rather than lingering.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: Can You Predict a Creative's Sales Potential in Advance?</h2>
<p>No 100% guarantee, but these methods improve hit rate:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Small-budget cold start testing</strong>: Allocate $5-$10 per creative for 24 hours. Compare CTR to CVR. High CTR paired with low CVR signals a creative that runs but won't sell.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Reference historical CVR benchmarks</strong>: Every category has a CVR average. Creatives performing 50%+ below average warrant quick elimination.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>User research validation</strong>: If possible, conduct small-scale surveys or interviews before full launch to confirm whether the creative's value proposition resonates with target users.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p><strong>'Running' lives in the upper funnel; 'selling' lives in the lower funnel.</strong></p>
<p>During creative reviews, don't be seduced by impressive click rates. Ask one question: Of the users this creative brings in, what percentage actually did what you wanted them to do?</p>
<p>Teams that can answer this clearly won't struggle with ROI.</p>
